[38;2;253;151;31mREGULAR EXPRESSIONS[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     A regular expression is a pattern that describes a set of strings.  Regular expressions are constructed analogously to arithmetic[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     expressions, by using various operators to combine smaller expressions.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     grep understands three different versions of regular expression syntax: “basic” (BRE), “extended” (ERE) and  “perl”  (PCRE).   In[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     GNU  grep, basic and extended regular expressions are merely different notations for the same pattern-matching functionality.  In[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     other implementations, basic regular expressions are ordinarily less powerful than extended, though occasionally it is the  other[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     way  around.   The  following  description applies to extended regular expressions; differences for basic regular expressions are[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     summarized afterwards.  Perl-compatible regular expressions have different functionality, and are  documented  in  [0m[38;2;166;226;46mpcre2syntax[0m[38;2;249;38;114m([0m[38;2;190;132;255m3[0m[38;2;249;38;114m)[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     and [0m[38;2;166;226;46mpcre2pattern[0m[38;2;249;38;114m([0m[38;2;190;132;255m3[0m[38;2;249;38;114m)[0m[38;2;248;248;242m, but work only if PCRE support is enabled.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     The  fundamental  building blocks are the regular expressions that match a single character.  Most characters, including all let‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     ters and digits, are regular expressions that match themselves.  Any meta-character with special meaning may be quoted by preced‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     ing it with a backslash.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     The period . matches any single character.  It is unspecified whether it matches an encoding error.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Character Classes and Bracket Expressions[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     A bracket expression is a list of characters enclosed by [ and ].  It matches any single character in that list.   If  the  first[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     character  of  the list is the caret ^ then it matches any character not in the list; it is unspecified whether it matches an en‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     coding error.  For example, the regular expression [0123456789] matches any single digit.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     Within a bracket expression, a range expression consists of two characters separated by a hyphen.  In the default  C  locale,  it[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     matches any single character that appears between the two characters in ASCII order, inclusive.  For example, [a-d] is equivalent[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     to  [abcd].  In other locales the behavior is unspecified: [a-d] might be equivalent to [abcd] or [aBbCcDd] or some other bracket[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     expression, or it might fail to match any character, or the set of characters that it matches might be erratic, or  it  might  be[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     invalid.   To  obtain the traditional interpretation of bracket expressions, you can use the C locale by setting the LC_ALL envi‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     ronment variable to the value C.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     Finally, certain named classes of characters are predefined within bracket expressions, as follows.  Their  names  are  self  ex‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     planatory,  and  they  are  [:alnum:],  [:alpha:],  [:blank:],  [:cntrl:], [:digit:], [:graph:], [:lower:], [:print:], [:punct:],[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     [:space:], [:upper:], and [:xdigit:].  For example, [[:alnum:]] means the character class of numbers and letters in  the  current[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     locale.   In  the  C  locale and ASCII character set encoding, this is the same as [0-9A-Za-z].  (Note that the brackets in these[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     class names are part of the symbolic names, and must be included in addition to the brackets delimiting the bracket  expression.)[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Most  meta-characters  lose their special meaning inside bracket expressions.  To include a literal ] place it first in the list.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Similarly, to include a literal ^ place it anywhere but first.  Finally, to include a literal - place it last.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Anchoring[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     The caret ^ and the dollar sign $ are meta-characters that respectively match the empty string at the  beginning  and  end  of  a[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     line.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   The Backslash Character and Special Expressions[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     The  symbols  \<  and \> respectively match the empty string at the beginning and end of a word.  The symbol \b matches the empty[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     string at the edge of a word, and \B matches the empty string provided it's not at the edge of a word.  The symbol \w is  a  syn‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     onym for [_[:alnum:]] and \W is a synonym for [^_[:alnum:]].[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Repetition[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     A regular expression may be followed by one of several repetition operators:[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     ?      The preceding item is optional and matched at most once.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     *      The preceding item will be matched zero or more times.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     +      The preceding item will be matched one or more times.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     {n}    The preceding item is matched exactly n times.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     {n,}   The preceding item is matched n or more times.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     {,m}   The preceding item is matched at most m times.  This is a GNU extension.[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     {n,m}  The preceding item is matched at least n times, but not more than m times.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Concatenation[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Two regular expressions may be concatenated; the resulting regular expression matches any string formed by concatenating two sub‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     strings that respectively match the concatenated expressions.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Alternation[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Two  regular  expressions may be joined by the infix operator |; the resulting regular expression matches any string matching ei‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     ther alternate expression.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Precedence[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Repetition takes precedence over concatenation, which in turn takes precedence over alternation.  A whole expression may  be  en‐[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     closed in parentheses to override these precedence rules and form a subexpression.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Back-references and Subexpressions[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     The back-reference \n, where n is a single digit, matches the substring previously matched by the nth parenthesized subexpression[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     of the regular expression.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Basic vs Extended Regular Expressions[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     In  basic  regular  expressions  the meta-characters ?, +, {, |, (, and ) lose their special meaning; instead use the backslashed[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     versions \?, \+, \{, \|, \(, and \).[0m

