[38;2;253;151;31mBUGS[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m   Reporting Bugs[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Email bug reports to the bug-reporting address.  An email archive and a bug tracker are available.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m   Known Bugs[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     Large  repetition counts in the {n,m} construct may cause grep to use lots of memory.  In addition, certain other obscure regular[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     expressions require exponential time and space, and may cause grep to run out of memory.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     Back-references are very slow, and may require exponential time.[0m

[38;2;253;151;31mEXAMPLE[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     The following example outputs the location and contents of any line containing “f” and ending in “.c”, within all  files  in  the[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     current directory whose names contain “g” and end in “.h”.  The [0m[38;2;166;226;46m-n[0m[38;2;248;248;242m option outputs line numbers, the -- argument treats expansions[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     of  “*g*.h” starting with “-” as file names not options, and the empty file /dev/null causes file names to be output even if only[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     one file name happens to be of the form “*g*.h”.[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m       $ grep [0m[38;2;166;226;46m-n[0m[38;2;248;248;242m -- 'f.*\.c$' *g*.h /dev/null[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m       argmatch.h:1:/* definitions and prototypes for argmatch.c[0m

[38;2;248;248;242m     The only line that matches is line 1 of argmatch.h.  Note that the regular expression syntax used in the pattern differs from the[0m
[38;2;248;248;242m     globbing syntax that the shell uses to match file names.[0m

